"""Grade 3 life essentials: meanness, enough, and attention."""
from spec import L, U

E3 = U('Living it every day', 'Morality', 'What do I do when someone is mean, when I want more, and when everything is calling for my attention?', [
L('When someone is mean to me',
  'Third graders meet unkindness every week: teasing, being left out, a pushed pencil case. They need more than “be nice.” They need to know that anger is a feeling, that forgiving is a choice that frees them, that they may stand up for themselves and for others, and that they should tell a grown-up when someone keeps hurting them. This lesson ties together the Catechism on anger, the Our Father, love of enemies and the works of mercy.',
  '2302-2303,2842-2845,1933,1829,2447,2288',
  'Matthew 5:43-48;Matthew 6:12-15;Luke 6:27-31;Proverbs 15:1',
  'forgiveness mercy peace dignity',
  'What is something someone did that hurt your feelings? What did you do?',
  'Practise the “Stop, Say, Tell” plan with a partner. Stop: take a breath. Say: use a strong, calm sentence (“Stop. I don’t like that.”). Tell: if it keeps happening, tell a trusted grown-up. Then practise one kind thing you could do for the person afterward, if it is safe.',
  'pater',
  id='3-L1',
  hook='Show two balloons, one blown up, one empty. Say, “Words are like air. They can fill someone up or let the air out.” Poke the pin in a pretend way, or let air out of one. Ask: “How does it feel when someone lets the air out of you?”',
  teach=[
    'Feeling angry or hurt is normal. Even Jesus got angry (Mark 3:5). The feeling is not a sin. What we do with it matters. The Church teaches that anger becomes a problem when we want to hurt someone back or hold on to it for a long time (CCC 2302–2303). Proverbs says, “A soft answer turns away wrath” (Proverbs 15:1). A calm voice is stronger than a loud one.',
    'Jesus taught something surprising: “Love your enemies and pray for those who persecute you” (Matthew 5:44). Loving does not mean liking, and it does not mean letting someone keep hurting you. It means wanting what is good for them, and not getting even. We pray this every day in the Our Father: “Forgive us our trespasses as we forgive those who trespass against us.” Forgiving is a way to set yourself free from carrying the hurt around.',
    'You are allowed to stand up for yourself and for others. You can say, calmly, “Stop. I don’t like that.” You can walk away. And you can tell a grown-up. Telling is not tattling when someone is being hurt or keeps hurting you. The Church says everyone has dignity and a right to be safe (CCC 2288, 1700). Telling helps keep people safe, including the one who is being mean.',
    'Jesus also shows us how to help. We can sit with the one who is left out. We can say something kind. We can pray for the person who was mean. The Church calls these works of mercy (CCC 2447). Prayer changes us, too: it is hard to stay angry at someone you are praying for.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'What is the difference between being angry and being mean?',
    'What does a “soft answer” sound like?',
    'When is it important to tell a grown-up?',
    'What is one kind thing you could do for someone who was mean to you?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'Is it a sin to feel angry?|Yes, always|No, but we must be careful what we do with it|Only on Sundays|Only if you shout|1|CCC 2302–2303.',
    'What does Jesus ask us to do for our enemies?|Get even|Love them and pray for them|Ignore them|Tell everyone|1|Matthew 5:44.',
    'When should you tell a grown-up?|Never|When someone keeps hurting you or someone else|Only for big things|Only if you are sure|1|CCC 2288.',
  ],
  life='This week practise “Stop, Say, Tell” in your head. When someone is unkind, take a slow breath first. Say one calm sentence. If it keeps happening, tell your teacher or a parent. And pray for the person once at night.',
  saint='Elizabeth of Hungary: a princess who treated everyone kindly, even when people at the castle were unkind to her.',
  misc=[
    '“Good Christians never get angry.”',
    '“Telling a grown-up is tattling.”',
    '“Forgiving means I have to be friends with them again.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:confession'],
  mem='“A soft answer turns away wrath.” (Proverbs 15:1)',
  assess='Ask each child to say the three steps of Stop, Say, Tell, and to say what forgiving means in their own words.',
  script=dict(
    open='Hold up two balloons, one full, one flat. Say, “Words are like air. Kind words fill you up. Unkind words let the air out.” Ask, “How does it feel when someone lets the air out of you?” Let two or three children answer.',
    teach=[
      'Say: “Everybody gets hurt feelings sometimes. And everybody gets angry. Jesus got angry in the Temple when people were being cheated. Feeling angry is not a sin. The question is: what do we do with it? Proverbs says, ‘A soft answer turns away wrath.’ A calm voice is stronger than a loud one.”',
      'Say: “Jesus said something surprising: love your enemies and pray for them. Does that mean you have to like them? No. Does it mean you let them keep hurting you? No. It means you want what is good for them, and you don’t try to get even. We say it every day in the Our Father: forgive us as we forgive. Forgiving sets you free from carrying the hurt.”',
      'Say: “You are allowed to stand up. You can say, calmly, ‘Stop. I don’t like that.’ You can walk away. And you can tell a grown-up. Telling is not tattling when someone is being hurt or keeps hurting you. Everyone has dignity and a right to be safe. Telling helps keep people safe, including the one who is being mean.”',
      'Say: “And you can help. You can sit with the one who is left out. You can say something kind. You can pray for the one who was mean. The Church calls these works of mercy. It is hard to stay angry at someone you are praying for.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'What makes you feel like a flat balloon? What fills you up?',
      'What is one calm sentence you could say?',
      'Who are the grown-ups you could tell?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Teach the three steps: Stop (breathe), Say (a calm sentence), Tell (a grown-up).',
      'In pairs, one child acts a pretend unkind moment (a pushed pencil case) and the other practises the three steps.',
      'Swap. Then each child draws one kind thing they could do afterward, if it is safe.',
    ],
    close='Pray the Our Father together, slowly. Say, “Soft words and strong hearts. That is how Jesus wants us to be.”'),
  tryit=['Learn Stop, Say, Tell.', 'Practise with a partner.', 'Draw one kind thing you could do.'],
  saintStory='Elizabeth was born in 1207, the daughter of the king of Hungary. At four she was sent to live at the castle of Wartburg in Germany, where she was to marry the young prince Louis. Some of the people in the castle did not like her, because she cared more for the poor than for fine clothes and fancy parties. They teased her and complained about her.\nShe married Louis when she was fourteen, and he loved her. They had three children. Elizabeth built a hospital below the castle, and gave bread to the hungry. A story says she was once carrying loaves of bread hidden in her cloak to the poor, when her husband stopped her and asked what she had. She said, “Roses.” He opened her cloak, and found roses.\nWhen Louis died on a crusade in 1227, her relatives drove her out of the castle with her children. She did not get angry or get even. She lived in a poor hut, and later served in a hospital she founded. She died in 1231 at only twenty-four, and was canonized four years later.',
),

L('Mine, yours, ours: sharing and enough',
  'Toys, snacks, space and turns are the first economics a child learns. The seventh and tenth commandments, the Church’s teaching on the goods of the earth and the Our Father’s “daily bread” give children a way of thinking: what I have is a gift, I do not need everything I want, I must not take what is not mine, and sharing makes more joy. The lesson makes these ideas playable.',
  '2401-2402,2408-2409,2443-2447,2544-2547,2828-2830',
  'Acts 20:35;Luke 3:11;Mark 12:41-44;John 6:1-13',
  'stewardship almsgiving justice gratitude',
  'What is something you really want right now? What would you do if you got it?',
  'Play “Enough”: give each group a small bag with unequal amounts of tokens or snacks. Ask: Is this fair? What could we do so that everyone has enough? Let them redistribute and say why. Then each child chooses one thing to share this week.',
  'before-meals',
  id='3-L2',
  hook='Give one child a handful of candy or tokens and others none. Ask the class what they feel. Then read about the boy with five loaves and two fish (John 6:9–13) and ask, “What if he had kept them to himself?”',
  teach=[
    'The earth and everything in it is a gift from God, meant for everybody. The Church calls this “the universal destination of goods” (CCC 2402). It means God made the world for all of us. Some things belong to us, our own toys and clothes, and we must respect what is someone else’s (CCC 2401, 2408). Taking something that is not ours is stealing, and it hurts trust. If you took something, the way back is to give it back and say sorry.',
    'Sharing is joy. Jesus said, “It is more blessed to give than to receive” (Acts 20:35). In the story of the loaves and fish, a boy gave his small lunch, and Jesus used it to feed five thousand people (John 6:1–13). Our gifts may seem small, but God can do a lot with a little. Jesus also noticed the widow who gave two small coins, because it was all she had (Mark 12:41–44).',
    'Wanting is normal, but wanting can grow too big. The tenth commandment asks us not to be greedy or jealous of what others have (CCC 2534–2540). A heart that is always wanting is never full. The first Beatitude, “Blessed are the poor in spirit,” means being happy with what we have and trusting God to give us what we need (CCC 2544–2547). Saying thank you every day helps us notice how much we already have.',
    'The Our Father says, “Give us this day our daily bread” (CCC 2828–2830). Notice the word “our,” not “my.” We ask for enough for today, and for everyone. When we share, we help answer that prayer. The Church calls helping the poor a work of mercy (CCC 2447), and even a child can do it, by sharing a snack, giving a toy, or saving a coin.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'What is something you shared that made you feel good?',
    'What do you do when you want something very much?',
    'What do you need to say or do if you took something that was not yours?',
    'Why does the Our Father say “our” bread?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What does “the universal destination of goods” mean?|The world is for rich people|God made the world as a gift for everyone|Everything belongs to the Pope|Nothing belongs to anyone|1|CCC 2402.',
    'What can you do if you took something that was not yours?|Hide it|Give it back and say sorry|Say nothing|Blame someone else|1|CCC 2409.',
    'What does Jesus say about giving?|It is more blessed to give than to receive|Never give|Only give old things|Only adults give|0|Acts 20:35.',
  ],
  life='This week choose one thing to share or give: a toy you do not use, part of your snack, time to play with someone. Also say thank you aloud for three things at dinner each night.',
  saint='Nicholas of Myra: a bishop who secretly gave gifts to a family in need, and who is the real Saint Nick.',
  misc=[
    '“If I share, I will have less.”',
    '“Wanting things means I am bad.”',
    '“Stealing is okay if it is small.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:tithing-and-money'],
  mem='“It is more blessed to give than to receive.” (Acts 20:35)',
  assess='Ask each child to say what “enough” means, name one thing they will share, and say what to do if they took something.',
  script=dict(
    open='Give one child a handful of tokens or candy and the others none. Ask the class, “How does this feel? Is it fair?” Then say, “Let’s hear a story about a boy who shared his lunch.” Tell John 6:9–13 briefly.',
    teach=[
      'Say: “God made the whole world as a gift for everyone. The Church calls this the universal destination of goods. Some things are ours, like our own toys and clothes. We must respect what is someone else’s. Taking something that isn’t ours is stealing, and it breaks trust. If you took something, give it back and say sorry. That is how you fix it.”',
      'Say: “Sharing is joy. Jesus said, ‘It is more blessed to give than to receive.’ A boy gave his small lunch, five loaves and two fish, and Jesus fed five thousand people. A widow gave two tiny coins, all she had, and Jesus noticed. Small gifts can do big things when God is in them.”',
      'Say: “Wanting is normal. But a heart that always wants more is never full. The tenth commandment asks us not to be greedy or jealous. Jesus said, ‘Blessed are the poor in spirit.’ That means being happy with what we have and trusting God. Saying thank you every day helps us notice what we already have.”',
      'Say: “In the Our Father we say, ‘Give us this day our daily bread.’ Notice: our. Not my. Enough for today, and for everyone. When we share, we help answer that prayer. The Church calls it a work of mercy. Even a child can do it: share a snack, give a toy, save a coin.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'What is something you have more than enough of? What could you share?',
      'How do you feel when you share? When you keep it all?',
      'What does “our daily bread” mean to you?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Play “Enough”: each group gets a bag of tokens, unequal amounts.',
      'Ask: How can we make sure everyone has enough? Let them move tokens and explain.',
      'Each child chooses one thing to share this week and draws it on a card.',
    ],
    close='Pray the grace before meals together. Say, “Say thank you, and share a little.”'),
  tryit=['Play Enough with tokens.', 'Choose one thing to share.', 'Draw it on a card.'],
  saintStory='Nicholas was born around the year 270 in a port town in what is now Turkey, to a wealthy Christian family. His parents died when he was young, and he used his inheritance to help people in need. He became the bishop of the city of Myra.\nA story is told about three sisters whose father was too poor to give them dowries, the money a bride needed to marry. Nicholas heard of their trouble. Late at night, he went to their house and tossed a bag of gold through the window. He did this three times, so that each sister had what she needed. The next morning the father learned that someone had given the gifts, and when he found it was Nicholas, the bishop asked him to keep it secret.\nNicholas was known for his kindness to children, sailors and the poor. He died around 343 and is remembered on December 6. Over the years his name became Sinterklaas in Dutch, and then Santa Claus.',
),

L('Attention: what gets my eyes and my ears',
  'Children at this age have screens, games, toys and noise everywhere. They are not too young to learn that attention is something you give, and that it shapes what you love. The Church’s teaching on prayer, rest, Sunday and temperance gives children simple practices: stop and look, be quiet with God, put the screen down for a time, and notice the person in front of you.',
  '2559,2697-2699,2729,2184-2185,1809,2186',
  'Psalm 46:10;Luke 10:38-42;Matthew 6:6;Mark 6:31',
  'prayer sabbath sunday temperance',
  'What is your favourite thing to do on a screen, and what is your favourite thing to do without one?',
  'Do a “quiet minute.” Sit still for sixty seconds with eyes closed and notice every sound. Then do a “look closely” walk: choose one thing in the room and describe five things about it. Finally, plan one “screen-free” time this week, and what you will do instead.',
  'morning-offering',
  id='3-L3',
  hook='Ring a small bell. Say, “Listen until you can’t hear it any more, then raise your hand.” Afterward ask: “What did you notice that you didn’t before? What happens when we really listen?”',
  teach=[
    'Attention is a gift we give. When you really look at someone, they feel loved. When you are always looking at something else, they feel left out. God pays attention to us, and prayer is paying attention to him. The Catechism says prayer is lifting up our mind and heart to God (CCC 2559). It does not need many words. A few quiet seconds can be a real prayer.',
    'Jesus visited the sisters Martha and Mary. Martha was busy and worried about many things, while Mary sat at Jesus’ feet and listened. Jesus said, “Martha, Martha, you are anxious and troubled about many things; one thing is needful. Mary has chosen the good portion” (Luke 10:41–42). He did not say that work is bad. He said that listening comes first. Psalm 46 says, “Be still, and know that I am God.”',
    'The Church also gives us a day of rest, Sunday. On Sunday we go to Mass, rest, play and spend time with family (CCC 2184–2186). Rest is not wasted time; it is part of how God made the world. Even God rested on the seventh day. Taking breaks from screens and noise helps our minds grow calm.',
    'Self-control, which the Church calls temperance, helps us use good things without being used by them (CCC 1809). Screens and games are not bad, but they can take over. It helps to decide ahead of time how long, to have times with no screens, and to notice how you feel afterward. If something you see online or on a screen frightens you or feels wrong, tell a grown-up right away.',
  ],
  talk=[
    'When someone really listens to you, how does it feel?',
    'What sounds do you hear when it is quiet?',
    'What could you do for fun instead of a screen?',
    'What should you do if you see something on a screen that frightens you?',
  ],
  quiz=[
    'What did Jesus say about Mary and Martha?|Mary was lazy|Mary chose the good portion by listening to him|Martha was bad|Both were wrong|1|Luke 10:41–42.',
    'What is temperance?|Hating screens|Self-control that uses good things without being used by them|Never having fun|Being strict|1|CCC 1809.',
    'What should you do if something online frightens you?|Hide it|Tell a grown-up right away|Show friends|Keep watching|1|CCC 2223; see also Matthew 18:10.',
  ],
  life='Pick one time this week that is screen-free: dinner, the first twenty minutes after school, or Sunday morning. Choose what you will do instead. Also take one “quiet minute” each night, just to say hello to God.',
  saint='Clare of Assisi: a young woman who gave up a rich life to spend her days in prayer, and who once held up the Eucharist and drove away an attacking army.',
  misc=[
    '“Screens are bad.”',
    '“Prayer needs long words.”',
    '“Resting is lazy.”',
  ],
  deeper=['apol:sunday-sabbath'],
  mem='“Be still, and know that I am God.” (Psalm 46:10)',
  assess='Ask each child to describe a quiet minute, say what they will do during a screen-free time, and say who they will tell if something frightens them.',
  script=dict(
    open='Ring a small bell and say, “Listen until you can’t hear it anymore, then raise your hand.” Afterward ask, “What did you notice that you didn’t before?” Say, “That is what attention does.”',
    teach=[
      'Say: “Attention is a gift. When you really look at someone, they feel loved. When you’re always looking at something else, they feel left out. God pays attention to us. Prayer is paying attention to him. A few quiet seconds can be a real prayer. It does not need many words.”',
      'Say: “Jesus visited two sisters, Martha and Mary. Martha was busy and worried. Mary sat and listened. Jesus said Mary had chosen the good portion. He did not say work is bad. He said listening comes first. Psalm 46 says, ‘Be still and know that I am God.’ Being still is not wasted. It lets us hear.”',
      'Say: “God gave us a day of rest, Sunday. We go to Mass, rest, play and spend time with family. Rest is not lazy; even God rested. Taking breaks from screens and noise helps our minds grow calm and our hearts grow bigger.”',
      'Say: “Self-control, called temperance, means using good things without being used by them. Screens and games aren’t bad, but they can take over. Decide ahead how long. Have screen-free times. Notice how you feel afterward. And if something frightens you or feels wrong, tell a grown-up right away. That is always the right thing.”',
    ],
    ask=[
      'What did you hear in the quiet that you didn’t hear before?',
      'What could you do with your family in a screen-free time?',
      'Who would you tell if something frightened you?',
    ],
    practice=[
      'Do a quiet minute together with eyes closed. Notice every sound.',
      'Do a “look closely” moment: choose an object and describe five things about it.',
      'Each child draws or writes one screen-free time this week and what they will do.',
    ],
    close='Pray the morning offering together. Say, “Pay attention to people, and to God. It is a gift.”'),
  tryit=['Take a quiet minute.', 'Look closely at one thing.', 'Plan a screen-free time.'],
  saintStory='Clare was born in 1193 into a noble family in Assisi, Italy. When she was eighteen, she heard Francis of Assisi preach and decided to give her life to God. On the night of Palm Sunday in 1212, she slipped out of her father’s house, met Francis and his friends, and had her hair cut off as a sign. She went to live in a small convent at San Damiano.\nClare and her sisters lived very simply, in poverty and prayer. They begged for food, and spent many hours in silence and praying. Clare became abbess against her wishes, and she wrote a rule of life for the sisters. She loved to gaze on the Eucharist. In 1240, when soldiers were about to attack the convent, a story says she held up the Blessed Sacrament at the window and prayed, and the soldiers fled.\nFor many years she was ill, and often in bed, but still she prayed and advised others. She died in 1253. The sisters are still called the Poor Clares. She was canonized in 1255, and later was named the patron of television, because when she was too ill to go to Mass, she is said to have seen and heard it on the wall of her room.',
),
])
